---
title: ACLs
order: 3
description: POSIX Access Control Lists — getfacl, setfacl
tags: [permissions, acl, getfacl, setfacl]
---

POSIX ACLs extend traditional Unix permissions by allowing fine-grained access rules for specific users and groups beyond the single owner/group/others model. Requires filesystem support (ext4, xfs, btrfs all support ACLs).

@text
Check and enable ACL support.

@code bash
# Check if filesystem is mounted with ACL support
mount | grep acl

# Most modern filesystems enable ACLs by default
# If not, remount with acl option
sudo mount -o remount,acl /dev/sda1 /mnt

# In fstab, add acl to options if needed (usually unnecessary for ext4/xfs)
# UUID=xxx  /data  ext4  defaults,acl  0  2

@text
getfacl — view Access Control Lists.

@code bash
# Show ACL for a file
getfacl file.txt

# Example output:
# # file: file.txt
# # owner: alice
# # group: users
# user::rw-
# user:bob:r--
# group::r--
# group:devs:rw-
# mask::rw-
# other::r--

# Show ACL for a directory
getfacl /shared/project

# Show ACL without comments (no owner/group header)
getfacl --omit-header file.txt

# Recursive
getfacl -R /shared/project

# Show only default ACL (directories)
getfacl -d /shared/project

# Tabular output
getfacl -t file.txt

# Backup ACLs to a file
getfacl -R /shared > acl-backup.txt

@text
setfacl — set Access Control Lists.

@code bash
# Grant a specific user read access
setfacl -m u:bob:r file.txt

# Grant a specific user read+write
setfacl -m u:bob:rw file.txt

# Grant a specific group read+write+execute
setfacl -m g:developers:rwx project/

# Set permissions for multiple entries at once
setfacl -m u:bob:rw,u:carol:r,g:devs:rwx file.txt

# Remove a specific user's ACL entry
setfacl -x u:bob file.txt

# Remove a specific group's ACL entry
setfacl -x g:developers file.txt

# Remove all ACL entries (revert to standard permissions)
setfacl -b file.txt

# Remove all default ACL entries from a directory
setfacl -k directory/

# Apply ACL recursively
setfacl -R -m g:developers:rwx /shared/project/

# Copy ACL from one file to another
getfacl source.txt | setfacl --set-file=- target.txt

# Restore ACLs from backup
setfacl --restore=acl-backup.txt

@text
Default ACLs — set on directories. New files and subdirectories automatically inherit these permissions.

@code bash
# Set default ACL on a directory (inherited by new files)
setfacl -d -m u:bob:rw /shared/project/
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /shared/project/

# Set both access and default ACL at once
setfacl -m u:bob:rw /shared/project/
setfacl -d -m u:bob:rw /shared/project/

# Show default ACL
getfacl -d /shared/project/

# Remove all default ACLs
setfacl -k /shared/project/

# Practical setup: shared project directory
sudo mkdir /shared/project
sudo chgrp developers /shared/project
sudo chmod 2770 /shared/project
sudo setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /shared/project/
sudo setfacl -d -m o::--- /shared/project/

@text
The ACL mask — the maximum effective permissions for named users, named groups, and the owning group. Automatically recalculated when you modify ACLs.

@code bash
# Set mask explicitly (limits all named user/group ACL entries)
setfacl -m m::rx file.txt

# After chmod on a file with ACLs, the mask may change
# chmod affects the mask, not the group entry
chmod 640 file.txt    # sets mask to r-- (may reduce effective ACL perms)

# Check effective permissions (shown in getfacl output)
getfacl file.txt
# user:bob:rw-    #effective:r--   <-- mask limits bob to r

@text
The '+' indicator in ls output signals ACLs are present.

@code bash
# A '+' after permissions indicates ACLs
ls -l file.txt
# -rw-r--r--+ 1 alice users 0 Jan 15 12:00 file.txt
#           ^ ACL present
